\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{3}z + \frac{7}{2} \cdot \frac{1}{4} &= \frac{3}{4}z + \frac{1}{2} \cdot \frac{1}{4} \\
\frac{1}{3}z + \frac{7}{8} &= \frac{3}{4}z + \frac{1}{8} \\
\end{align*}

Subtract $\frac{1}{3}z$ from both sides:

\begin{align*}
\frac{7}{8} &= \frac{3}{4}z - \frac{1}{3}z \\
\end{align*}

Convert $\frac{3}{4}z$ and $\frac{1}{3}z$ to a common denominator, which is 12:

\begin{align*}
\frac{7}{8} &= \frac{9}{12}z - \frac{4}{12}z \\
\frac{7}{8} &= \frac{5}{12}z \\
\end{align*}

Next, isolate $z$ by multiplying both sides by $\frac{12}{5}$:

\begin{align*}
z &= \frac{7}{8} \cdot \frac{12}{5} \\
z &= \frac{21}{10} \\
z &= \frac{21}{10} \\
\end{align*}

